PART 1: this Keyword
1. What is this?
this is a reference variable that points to the current object.

Interview one-liner
“this refers to the current invoking object.”

2. Uses of this Keyword (All Possibilities)
  2.1 Referring to Instance Variables (Most Common)
  Example
  class Person {
      int age;
  
      void setAge(int age) {
          this.age = age;
      }
  
      void show() {
          System.out.println(age);
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          Person p = new Person();
          p.setAge(35);
          p.show();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  35

👉 Used to resolve variable shadowing

2.2 Calling Current Class Method
  class Demo {
      void m1() {
          System.out.println("m1 method");
      }
  
      void m2() {
          this.m1(); // optional
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Demo().m2();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  m1 method

2.3 Constructor Chaining Using this()
  Example
  class Employee {
  
      Employee() {
          this(101);
          System.out.println("Default constructor");
      }
  
      Employee(int id) {
          System.out.println("Parameterized constructor: " + id);
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Employee();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  Parameterized constructor: 101
  Default constructor

Rules
this() must be first statement
Avoids code duplication

2.4 Passing this as Method Argument
    class TestThis {
        void display(TestThis obj) {
            System.out.println("Method called");
        }
    
        void call() {
            display(this);
        }
    }
    
    public class Test {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            new TestThis().call();
        }
    }
    
    Output
    Method called

2.5 Returning this (Method Chaining)
    class Builder {
        Builder setA() {
            System.out.println("setA");
            return this;
        }
    
        Builder setB() {
            System.out.println("setB");
            return this;
        }
    }
    
    public class Test {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            new Builder().setA().setB();
        }
    }
    
    Output
    setA
    setB

👉 Used in Builder pattern

2.6 this in Static Context ❌
class Test {
    static void show() {
        // System.out.println(this); ❌
    }
}
❌ Static methods don’t belong to objects
