PART 2: super Keyword
3. What is super?
super is a reference to the immediate parent class object.

Interview one-liner
“super refers to the parent class part of the current object.”

4. Uses of super Keyword
  4.1 Access Parent Class Variable
  class Parent {
      int x = 10;
  }
  
  class Child extends Parent {
      int x = 20;
  
      void show() {
          System.out.println(x);
          System.out.println(super.x);
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Child().show();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  20
  10

4.2 Call Parent Class Method
  class Parent {
      void display() {
          System.out.println("Parent display");
      }
  }
  
  class Child extends Parent {
      void display() {
          super.display();
          System.out.println("Child display");
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Child().display();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  Parent display
  Child display

4.3 Call Parent Class Constructor (super())
  class Parent {
      Parent(int x) {
          System.out.println("Parent constructor: " + x);
      }
  }
  
  class Child extends Parent {
      Child() {
          super(50);
          System.out.println("Child constructor");
      }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new Child();
      }
  }

  Output
  Parent constructor: 50
  Child constructor

Rules
super() must be first statement
If not written, compiler inserts default super()

5. Constructor Chaining Order (Very Important)
  class A {
      A() { System.out.println("A"); }
  }
  
  class B extends A {
      B() { System.out.println("B"); }
  }
  
  class C extends B {
      C() { System.out.println("C"); }
  }
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          new C();
      }
  }
  
  Output
  A
  B
  C


👉 Parent constructor always executes first

6. this vs super (🔥 Must-Know Table)
| Feature          | `this`         | `super`       |
| ---------------- | -------------- | ------------- |
| Refers to        | Current object | Parent object |
| Variables        | Current class  | Parent class  |
| Methods          | Current class  | Parent class  |
| Constructor call | `this()`       | `super()`     |
| Static context   | ❌              | ❌             |

7. Rules & Restrictions (Tricky)
  ❌ this() and super() cannot be together
  ❌ Cannot use super in static context
  ❌ Cannot access private parent members via super
  ✔ this can be returned

8. this and super with Static Context
  class Test {
      static void m1() {
          // this ❌
          // super ❌
      }
  }
  👉 Static methods belong to class, not object.

9. JVM / Memory Perspective
    this → reference to current heap object
    super → reference to parent part of same object
    Only one object is created (child)
    Parent variables are part of child object

10. this & super in Spring Boot (Important)
  Dependency Injection & this
  @Service
  class UserService {
  
      @Autowired
      private Repo repo;
  
      void process() {
          this.repo.save(); // optional
      }
  }
this rarely required
Used mainly for clarity or method chaining

12. Common Tricky Interview Questions (4+ Years)
Q1. Can this be null?
❌ No — it always refers to current object

Q2. Can we use super.super?
❌ No — only immediate parent

Q3. Is super() mandatory?
✔ Implicitly added if parent has default constructor

Q4. Can we override constructor?
❌ No — constructors are not inherited

Q5. Why super() must be first?
To ensure parent initialization before child

Q6. Difference between this.x and x?
No difference unless variable shadowing exists
